<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Pawmodoro — macOS entitlements (Mac App Store).

  This file is for the macOS build ONLY. It is wired up as
      CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS[sdk=macosx*] = Pawmodoro/Mac/Pawmodoro.entitlements
  and the iOS build takes Pawmodoro/iOS/Pawmodoro.entitlements instead, so
  neither platform inherits the other's claims. App Sandbox is a macOS
  concept; handing this to an iOS build is at best noise.

  The list below is deliberately one key long. Every entitlement is a sentence
  App Review reads and a claim the privacy policy has to survive, and this app
  genuinely does nothing that needs a second one:

    - No network. The app makes zero network calls (docs/PRIVACY.md), so
      com.apple.security.network.client and .network.server are both absent.
      See MAC_APP_STORE.md § "The one entitlement question I could not settle"
      before adding network.client for StoreKit — test first, do not add it
      speculatively.
    - No file access. The Scrapbook imports through SwiftUI's PhotosPicker,
      which runs out of process and hands back Data; nothing here opens a file
      by path. So no com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only and no
      com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library. If a Mac-only
      NSOpenPanel import is ever added, user-selected.read-only is the key it
      needs — and only that one, read-only.
    - No camera. Pawmodoro/Views/CameraPicker.swift is fenced behind
      canImport(UIKit) and does not exist in the Mac build, so
      com.apple.security.device.camera stays out even though the shared
      Info.plist still carries NSCameraUsageDescription.
    - No microphone, no location, no address book, no calendar, no printing,
      no Bluetooth, no USB.
    - No App Groups — on macOS only. TimerEngine writes two values to a
      "group.com.pawmodoro" suite for the home-screen widget, and the iOS
      build DOES carry that entitlement (Pawmodoro/iOS/Pawmodoro.entitlements
      and PawmodoroWidgets/PawmodoroWidgets.entitlements). The widget
      extension is iOS-only, though (project.pbxproj carries platformFilter =
      ios on both its dependency and its embed phase), so on macOS that write
      has no reader. Without the entitlement it lands in the app's own
      container, which is harmless and is what the code comment there already
      says. Note too that a macOS app-group identifier must be prefixed with
      the team ID (6YFQ69HSD6.group.com.pawmodoro), so the iOS string would
      not even be valid here. Leave it out until something on the Mac reads it.

  What needs no entitlement, and is worth writing down so nobody adds one
  "just in case":
    - UserDefaults, and the app's own Documents directory (Snapshot.swift),
      are inside the sandbox container already.
    - AVAudioEngine playback of bundled files.
    - UNUserNotificationCenter local notifications.
    - StoreKit purchases and restore — the store UI and the transaction work
      happen in Apple's own process. (Unverified on this app; see the doc.)
    - ShareLink / NSSharingService — the share sheet is out of process.

  Do NOT add com.apple.security.get-task-allow here. Xcode injects it for
  debugging and strips it when archiving; a copy in this file would ship in the
  archive and the upload is rejected.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Mandatory for the Mac App Store. Nothing else in this file. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
